## [GAP ANALYSIS] Same NPE pattern left in the paginated `getSubGroupsStream` overloads

**Severity:** MEDIUM — a concurrent group delete can still produce a 500 from the admin REST API

### The Problem

The PR guards `getSubGroupsCount()` against the lazy DB model being `null`:

```java
GroupModel model = modelSupplier.get();
return model == null ? null : model.getSubGroupsCount();
```

The three sibling overloads right above it (lines 256, 262, 268) still use the old pattern:

```java
return modelSupplier.get().getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, firstResult, maxResults);
```

`modelSupplier` is a `LazyModel` over `getGroupModel()`, which calls `cacheSession.getGroupDelegate().getGroupById(realm, cached.getId())`. If another transaction deleted the group after this `GroupAdapter` was built from the cache, that lookup returns `null`. That is exactly why `getSubGroupsCount()` threw before this PR.

### Theory / Proof

`GroupResource.getSubGroups` (`services/.../admin/GroupResource.java:180`) calls `group.getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, -1, -1)` on the `GroupModel` that `GroupsResource.getGroupById` resolved at the start of the request. If the group is deleted between that lookup and the delegate load, `modelSupplier.get()` returns `null` and the call throws a NullPointerException, which becomes an HTTP 500. The other methods on this adapter already handle a missing DB entity: `CachedGroup`'s `DefaultLazyLoader` uses a fallback when the source is `null`, and `getRoleMappings` checks for `null`. These three methods do not.

### What happens today vs. what will break

The listing path from issue #40368 (`GET /groups` -> `populateSubGroupCount`) is fixed. Browsing children of a group that is being deleted (`GET /groups/{id}/children`) still has the same race.

### Research

Other adapter methods here fall back to an empty collection when the source model is missing (`DefaultLazyLoader` fallback `Collections::emptySet`), so returning `Stream.empty()` matches the existing behaviour.

### Recommendation

Apply the same guard to all three overloads:

```java
@Override
public Stream<GroupModel> getSubGroupsStream(String search, Boolean exact, Integer firstResult, Integer maxResults) {
    if (isUpdated()) return updated.getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, firstResult, maxResults);
    GroupModel model = modelSupplier.get();
    return model == null ? Stream.empty() : model.getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, firstResult, maxResults);
}
```
(Do the same for the `(search, first, max)` and `(first, max)` overloads.)

## [ISSUE] Regression test can pass without exercising the race

**Severity:** LOW — the test is non-deterministic and may not catch a regression

### The Problem

```java
new Thread(() -> {
    while (!deletedAll.get()) { ... groups(null, 0, Integer.MAX_VALUE, true) ... }
}).start();

groupUuuids.forEach(groupUuid -> managedRealm.admin().groups().group(groupUuid).remove());
deletedAll.set(true);

assertThat(caughtExceptions, Matchers.empty());
```

1. The main thread asserts right after `deletedAll.set(true)` without `join()`-ing the reader. If the reader is inside `groups(...)` at that moment and that request fails, the exception is added to `caughtExceptions` after the assertion has already run. That request is the one most likely to overlap the final deletes.
2. Nothing checks that the reader ran at least one request while deletes were in progress. If thread startup is slow, or the 100 deletes finish quickly, the loop may run zero or one iterations, and the test passes whether or not the fix is present.

### Theory / Proof

`Thread.start()` does not guarantee when the new thread gets scheduled. Without a happens-before edge from `join()`, the main thread may also not see writes the reader makes to the list after the assertion (the `CopyOnWriteArrayList` publishes each write, but the assertion can run before the write).

### Recommendation

Join the reader thread and assert that it actually overlapped the deletes:

```java
AtomicInteger reads = new AtomicInteger();
Thread reader = new Thread(() -> {
    while (!deletedAll.get()) {
        try {
            managedRealm.admin().groups().groups(null, 0, Integer.MAX_VALUE, true);
            reads.incrementAndGet();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            caughtExceptions.add(e);
        }
    }
});
reader.start();
groupUuuids.forEach(id -> managedRealm.admin().groups().group(id).remove());
deletedAll.set(true);
reader.join(10_000);
assertThat(caughtExceptions, Matchers.empty());
assertThat(reads.get(), Matchers.greaterThan(0));
```
